1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:06,890
The upper piece is lowered onto
the lower, and the pegs go in.

2
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:12,290
This time we mark one
piece out and cut it,

3
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:16,430
step by step, following
one training course.

4
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:20,650
The course marks and cuts
the lower piece first.

5
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:26,150
The upper piece is the same shape,
but its slope runs the other way.

6
00:00:31,333 --> 00:00:33,903
Marking starts on the top face:

7
00:00:34,533 --> 00:00:37,833
the end line, then the
line for the housing

8
00:00:37,873 --> 00:00:40,343
that will take the other piece's tongue.

9
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:46,240
Next, the shoulder lines and
the line along the beam, then

10
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:49,750
the other shoulder, with
marks to locate the slope.

11
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:55,400
Using those marks, the slope
line goes on the top face and

12
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:59,290
on one side face, with
that face's shoulder lines.

13
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:03,430
The underside is then marked
the same way as the top.

14
00:01:03,767 --> 00:01:08,487
On the other side face goes the
splice's center line, and the

15
00:01:08,527 --> 00:01:12,577
peg center lines are set out
evenly either side of it.

16
00:01:13,407 --> 00:01:17,027
For some of the peg
lines, the course uses the

17
00:01:17,067 --> 00:01:20,757
sashigane's blade width as a quick gauge.

18
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:26,830
Cutting the lower piece.

19
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:33,290
Cut the end at the end line,
and mark the end grain.

20
00:01:33,667 --> 00:01:37,017
Most of these cuts leave half the line:

21
00:01:37,727 --> 00:01:40,307
the edge of the cut falls
on the middle of the

22
00:01:40,347 --> 00:01:44,517
ink line, so half of
it stays on the piece.

23
00:01:45,727 --> 00:01:47,377
Episode ten did the same.

24
00:01:47,733 --> 00:01:52,173
Before sawing the joint, the
course chops the peg holes

25
00:01:52,213 --> 00:01:56,443
from the side face, about a
quarter of the width deep.

26
00:01:56,867 --> 00:02:02,467
Then it rips along the beam
from the end, leaving half the

27
00:02:02,507 --> 00:02:08,357
line, to just past the slope,
and crosscuts at the slope.

28
00:02:09,087 --> 00:02:12,217
The first block of waste comes away.

29
00:02:12,533 --> 00:02:16,443
It crosscuts the shoulder,
leaving half the line,

30
00:02:16,833 --> 00:02:20,343
down to just short of
the line along the beam.

31
00:02:21,113 --> 00:02:25,053
Then it drives a chisel into
the end grain at the slope

32
00:02:25,093 --> 00:02:29,023
cut, and splits that waste
off, back to the shoulder.

33
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:33,323
Mark the slope again,
on the newly split face.

34
00:02:34,393 --> 00:02:39,683
Saw it, leaving half the line, to
just short of the line along the beam.

35
00:02:40,373 --> 00:02:44,473
Then add kerfs about a
chisel's width apart, back

36
00:02:44,513 --> 00:02:47,763
to the shoulder, careful
not to saw too deep.

37
00:02:48,653 --> 00:02:52,243
Chop them out, and pare,
leaving half the line.

38
00:02:52,567 --> 00:02:55,477
Next, the housing at the shoulder:

39
00:02:55,687 --> 00:02:58,237
kerfs, then the chisel.

40
00:02:59,287 --> 00:03:02,977
Then the tongue that goes into
the other piece's housing:

41
00:03:03,627 --> 00:03:07,897
a rip and a crosscut,
leaving half the line.

42
00:03:08,233 --> 00:03:14,243
Last, it marks the peg holes on
the newly cut faces and chops them.

43
00:03:14,533 --> 00:03:20,023
The upper piece is the same shape, cut
with its slope running the other way.

44
00:03:24,067 --> 00:03:28,427
Then the upper piece is
lowered onto the lower,

45
00:03:28,467 --> 00:03:32,297
and the two pegs go in
from opposite sides.

46
00:03:32,733 --> 00:03:39,883
One test database warns against a
fit so tight it has to be driven in.

47
00:03:40,453 --> 00:03:44,913
It suggests a fit where light
taps bring the two pieces

48
00:03:44,953 --> 00:03:48,723
level, with the pegs there
to stop them slipping.

49
00:03:51,433 --> 00:03:53,303
That's the end of this series:

50
00:03:53,813 --> 00:03:58,033
from the story stick and the
marking kit, through the saws and

51
00:03:58,073 --> 00:04:03,203
chisels, to three splices, a
crossing joint and a tenon.

52
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:13,640
Mark the lower piece first, chop the
peg holes early, take the waste off in

53
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:20,830
stages, cut the slope, and make the
upper the same, with its slope reversed.

54
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:31,885
One note:

55
00:04:31,925 --> 00:04:37,120
sharp-tool technique, and how
to use these tools safely, is

56
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:40,770
best learned from a teacher,
at a bench, in person.
